    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s longest-ruling dynasty, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a> in 1392 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a>, today's Seoul.</li><li>A model <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucian</a> state run by examination-trained yangban officials; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Korean alphabet</a> in 1443.</li><li>It survived Japanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> invasions but was forced open by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1876 and became the Korean Empire in 1897.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a> record 472 years of government day by day, written by court historians whose drafts even the king was not supposed to read.</p></aside>
    </section>

