    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon.jpg" alt="Taejo of Joseon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Taejo of Joseon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Taejo_Yi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jo Jung-muk(?-?), Pak Gijun(?-?), Baek Eunbae(1820-?), Yu Suk(1827-1873), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Joseon was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a>, a general from the northeastern frontier who had won fame fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-turban-rebellion">Red Turban</a> rebels and Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> in the last decades of Goryeo. In 1388, ordered to attack the Ming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, he turned his army back at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihwa-island">Wihwa Island</a> and seized control of the government. Allied with reform-minded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucian</a> officials led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeong-do-jeon">Jeong Do-jeon</a>, he carried out a land reform in 1391, the Rank Land Law (<em>gwajeon</em>), which broke the estates of the old aristocracy and endowed his supporters. In 1392 he deposed the last Goryeo king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongyang-of-goryeo">Gongyang</a>, and took the throne; he is known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goguryeo">Taejo</a> (r. 1392–1398). In 1393 the Ming emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu</a> approved the name Joseon, recalling the ancient kingdom of Gojoseon, and the dynasty accepted a tributary relationship with the Ming. In 1394 the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a>, renamed Hanseong and today Seoul, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a> palace and city walls were built. The early dynasty was violent: in 1398 Taejo's fifth son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejong-of-joseon">Yi Bang-won</a>, killed Jeong Do-jeon and two half-brothers in the first 'Strife of Princes', and in 1400 he became king as Taejong (r. 1400–1418). Taejong abolished private armies, introduced identity tags (<em>hopae</em>) for adult men and organised the country into eight provinces, consolidating royal power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg" alt="Beijing" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Beijing_CBD_with_B-5906_approaching_(20211016171955)_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">N509FZ, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Joseon society was strictly hierarchical. At the top stood the <em>yangban</em>, the families of civil and military officials, who dominated the examinations and office and held much of the land. Below them were the <em>jungin</em>, hereditary specialists such as interpreters, physicians and clerks; the commoners (<em>sangmin</em>), mostly farmers who bore taxes, labour and military service; and the lowborn (<em>cheonmin</em>), above all slaves (<em>nobi</em>), who in the early centuries may have made up a third or more of the population. Agriculture, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, was the basis of the economy. The state promoted farming manuals such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nongsa-jikseol">Nongsa jikseol</a></em> of 1429, and rice transplanting spread widely in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. The Uniform Land Tax Law (<em>daedongbeop</em>), introduced in stages from 1608 and extended across the country by 1708, replaced tribute in local goods with a tax in rice, which stimulated markets and licensed merchants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangpyeong-tongbo">Sangpyeong tongbo</a></em>, circulated from 1678. Foreign trade was tightly limited to tribute missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and the Japanese trading post at Busan, run by the Sō clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsushima-island">Tsushima</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> reshaped family life: from the seventeenth century inheritance increasingly favoured the eldest son, daughters lost their share of property, and ancestral rites centred on the male line. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> lost state support, and its monks were long barred from the capital.</p></div><p class="profile-ladder"><a href="/societies/joseon">The social ladder of Joseon Korea &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myeongnyang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-myeongnyang.jpg" alt="Battle of Myeongnyang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myeongnyang">Battle of Myeongnyang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navalzhugenu2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">太田天洋(1884～1946), CC BY 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Early Joseon fought mainly on its frontiers. In 1419, early in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong</a>'s reign, the retired king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejong-of-joseon">Taejong</a> sent an expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsushima-island">Tsushima</a> to suppress Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> in the 1430s and 1440s established the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> rivers, where it still lies. A long peace followed, and by the 1590s the army was neglected. In 1592 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> invaded with some 150,000 men; Busan fell at once, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> within three weeks, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seonjo-of-joseon">Seonjo</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uiju">Uiju</a> on the Yalu. The tide turned at sea, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-sun-sin">Admiral Yi</a> Sun-sin, using armoured turtle ships (<em>geobukseon</em>) and panokseon warships, won victories such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hansan-island">Battle of Hansan Island</a> in 1592 that cut Japanese supply lines, while 'righteous armies' (<em>uibyeong</em>) of volunteers harassed the invaders and Ming armies retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 1593. A second invasion in 1597 was contained; Yi won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-myeongnyang">Myeongnyang</a> with thirteen ships and was killed at the final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-noryang">Battle of Noryang</a> in 1598. Reforms after the war created the Military Training Agency and the Five Army Camps, but they could not stop the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, who invaded in 1627 and again in 1636–1637, forcing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/injo-of-joseon">Injo</a> to surrender after a siege at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namhansanseong">Namhansanseong</a>. In the nineteenth century Joseon forces fought off French and American expeditions in 1866 and 1871.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty.jpg" alt="Annals of the Joseon Dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseon_Wangjo_Sillok_and_its_case_in_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Salamander724, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Joseon was one of the most thoroughly Confucian states in history. The king ruled with the State Council (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-council-of-joseon">Uijeongbu</a></em>) and six ministries, but his power was checked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/censorate">censorate</a>, whose offices criticised royal conduct and official abuses, by the royal lectures at which kings studied the classics with scholars, and by court historians whose records became the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a></em>. Officials were recruited through civil and military examinations held throughout the dynasty, and the country was divided into eight provinces under governors, with county magistrates rotated so that they never served in their home districts. The legal basis was the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongguk-daejeon">Gyeongguk daejeon</a></em> (National Code), begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejo-of-joseon">Sejo</a> and promulgated in 1485 under Seongjong. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">Sejong the Great</a> (r. 1418–1450) gathered scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-worthies">Hall of Worthies</a> (<em>Jiphyeonjeon</em>) and sponsored works on agriculture, medicine, astronomy and history. Royal authority was often contested: Sejo usurped the throne from his nephew Danjong in 1455, and the literati purges of 1498, 1504, 1519 and 1545 set kings and established officials against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucian</a> scholars. From the 1570s factional rivalry divided the bureaucracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon">Yeongjo</a> (r. 1724–1776) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">Jeongjo</a> (r. 1776–1800) tried to balance the factions through a 'policy of impartiality' (<em>tangpyeong</em>), but in the nineteenth century royal in-law families, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong-kim-clan">Andong Kim</a>, dominated government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum.jpg" alt="Hunminjeongeum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunminjeongum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sejong the Great of Joseon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Joseon's most celebrated achievement is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Korean alphabet</a>. In 1443 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejong-the-great">King Sejong</a> created a script of 28 letters, promulgated in 1446 as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunminjeongeum">Hunminjeongeum</a></em> ('Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People'), so that ordinary people could learn to write their own language. Long disdained by the literati, who wrote in classical Chinese, it became the national script of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as Hangul. Sejong's reign also produced the rain gauge of 1441, water clocks and astronomical instruments developed with the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-yeong-sil">Jang Yeong-sil</a>, and books printed with improved metal type. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-the-joseon-dynasty">Annals of the Joseon Dynasty</a></em> and the <em>Diaries of the Royal Secretariat</em> are among the most detailed state records of any premodern country and are inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World</a> register. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucian</a> philosophy reached a high level in the debates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a> (Toegye) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-i">Yi I</a> (Yulgok), whose ideas also influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and later scholars of Practical Learning (<em>silhak</em>) such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeong-yak-yong">Jeong Yak-yong</a> turned to the reform of land, administration and technology. Joseon's art, from white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and buncheong ware to the genre paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-hong-do">Kim Hong-do</a>, defines much of what is seen today as traditional Korean culture, and Neo-Confucian family values shaped Korean society long after the dynasty ended.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Inventions and discoveries credited here</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/inventions?focus=hwacha">Hwacha<span>1409</span></a><a href="/inventions?focus=turtle-ship">Turtle ship<span>1413</span></a><a href="/inventions?focus=hangul">Hangul<span>1443</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imo-incident.jpg" alt="Imo Incident" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident">Imo Incident</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imogullan1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">歌川国松, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wars of the 1590s and 1630s devastated Joseon, and the submission to the Qing in 1637 left lasting resentment, though Joseon remained a dutiful Qing tributary. The eighteenth-century revival under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yeongjo-of-joseon">Yeongjo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongjo-of-joseon">Jeongjo</a>, which included the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaseong-fortress">Hwaseong Fortress</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwon">Suwon</a>, was followed by the rule of in-law clans, corrupt taxation and recurring famines and epidemics, which provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-gyeong-naes-rebellion">Hong Gyeong-nae rebellion</a> of 1811–1812 and widespread peasant risings in 1862. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, spreading from the late eighteenth century, was harshly persecuted, and in 1860 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-je-u">Choe Je-u</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak">Donghak</a> ('Eastern Learning') religion. The regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Heungseon Daewongun</a>, father of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Gojong</a>, strengthened the throne from 1864 and repelled French and American expeditions, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> forced the country open with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa">Treaty of Ganghwa</a> in 1876. Joseon became a battleground for Chinese, Japanese and Russian influence: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident">Imo Incident</a> of 1882 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gapsin-coup">Gapsin Coup</a> of 1884 brought Chinese and Japanese troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. In 1894 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghak-peasant-revolution">Donghak Peasant Revolution</a> prompted both powers to intervene, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>. Japan's victory ended Chinese suzerainty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> of 1895; Japanese agents murdered Queen Min that year, and Gojong took refuge in the Russian legation in 1896. In October 1897 Gojong proclaimed the Korean Empire, ending the kingdom of Joseon; Japan annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1910.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Joseon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s longest-ruling dynasty, governed the peninsula for five centuries as a model <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucian</a> state, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> alphabet and an unrivalled record of government, survived Japanese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> invasions, and shaped the family values, culture and identity of modern Korea before falling under Japanese pressure.</em></p>

